@@s001 Java for Kattis
@@s002 dato:value,ora:operation,no:wrong value (overflow),ok:correct with long
@@s003 An <code>int</code> has 32 bits: the maximum is 2147483647 (<code>Integer.MAX_VALUE</code>). Java gives no error when it is exceeded: it <b>overflows</b>.
@@s004 I add 1: the result no longer fits and restarts from the minimum. The value becomes <b>−2147483648</b>, negative.
@@s005 The same happens with subtraction in comparators: <code>a - b</code> can give a wrong result with large values. Use <code>Integer.compare(a, b)</code>.
@@s006 If the sum can exceed 2 billion (sums of weights, costs, distances) use <code>long</code>: 64 bits. The literal is written with L: <code>1000000000L</code>.
@@s007 Java for Kattis
@@s008 Kattis judges correctness and time: a correct algorithm can get 'Time Limit Exceeded' if it reads the input slowly or gets the data types wrong. This page explains the language you need, with the measurements made on this PC.
@@s009 Reading the input quickly
@@s010 <code>Scanner</code> is convenient but slow: it parses each token with regular expressions. For large inputs use <code>BufferedReader</code>, which reads one line at a time, and <code>StringTokenizer</code> to split it. Measured on 1 000 000 lines: <code>Scanner.nextInt()</code> about 770 ms, <code>BufferedReader + StringTokenizer</code> about 200 ms.
@@s011 The name of the public class must match the name of the file, and <code>readLine()</code> can throw <code>IOException</code>: you declare it with <code>throws IOException</code>.
@@s012 Numeric types and overflow
@@s013 <code>int</code> (32 bits) goes from −2³¹ to 2³¹ − 1. When it exceeds the limit there is no error: the value restarts from the other side. <code>long</code> (64 bits) is the choice for sums of weights and costs. For comparisons use <code>Integer.compare</code> instead of subtraction.
@@s014 int exceeding the maximum
@@s015 Arrays, lists and collections
@@s016 Arrays (<code>int[]</code>) have a fixed length and default values 0 (or <code>false</code>, or <code>null</code>). Lists (<code>ArrayList</code>) grow; a queue is an <code>ArrayDeque</code>: <code>add</code> at the back, <code>poll</code> at the front. A priority queue (<code>PriorityQueue</code>) always comes out with the minimum according to a comparator.
@@s017 Lambdas and comparators
@@s018 A comparator is a function that says whether one element comes before another. In Java it is written as a lambda: <code>(x, y) -> Integer.compare(x[1], y[1])</code> sorts by column 1. The lambda is an unnamed function passed as an argument.
@@s019 The code, side by side
@@s020 This page explains the concept. The Java code that implements it, with a line-by-line explanation and the syntax notes needed, is on the side-by-side page.
